# Code Standards & Principles

## Simplicity

- Before writing any code, stop at the first rung that holds:
    1. Does this need to be built at all? (YAGNI)
    2. Does it already exist in this codebase? Reuse the helper, util, or pattern that's already here, don't re-write it.
    3. Does the standard library already do this? Use it.
    4. Does a native platform feature cover it? Use it.
    5. Does an already-installed dependency solve it? Use it.
    6. Can this be one line? Make it one line.
    7. Only then: write the minimum code that works.
- Understand end-to-end first: Read the task and touched code, trace the real flow before choosing an approach.
- Shortest working diff wins once understood. Deletion over addition. Boring over clever. Fewest files possible.
- No abstractions, boilerplate, or dependencies that were not explicitly requested.
- Question complex requests: Propose simpler alternatives if a lighter approach covers the need.
- Between equal-sized approaches, pick the edge-case-correct algorithm over the flimsy one.
- If intentionally simplifying with a known ceiling (e.g. global lock, O(n²) scan, naive heuristic), mark with a comment stating the ceiling and upgrade path.

## Root Cause & Quality Boundaries

- Fix root causes across all callers; grep every caller of a touched function and fix shared logic once rather than patching symptoms per call site.
- Never cut corners on: input validation at trust boundaries, error handling that prevents data loss, security, accessibility, and hardware/platform calibrations.
- Fail fast with clear, actionable error messages.
- Non-trivial logic must leave behind the smallest runnable check that fails if the logic breaks (e.g. assert-based self-check or focused test; no extra frameworks or fixtures). Trivial one-liners need no test.

## Naming, Scoping & Documentation

- Scope constants as locally as practical; avoid module-level constants used in only one function.
- Use descriptive names that reveal intent (e.g. `fetch_active_agents()` not `get_data()`). Avoid `resolve`, `normalize`, or `interpolate` when a clearer verb or noun exists.
- Document every function with docstrings (parameters, important constraints, return values, errors thrown).
- Use single-line inline comments to explain the "what" and "why" (context, assumptions, edge cases) of non-obvious logic.

## Third-Party Libraries

- Consult installed version docs (README, API reference, local types) and adhere to recommended usage and lifecycles.
- Prefer documented library APIs and control flow directly; do not re-implement or wrap what the library already provides.
- Trust and surface library errors unless a domain requirement explicitly demands mapping.
- If a recommended pattern requires an unsupported feature in the pinned version, document the workaround reason with a comment.
